Hi - I finally figured out that dplyr::do isn't as intuitive as I'd thought.  My typical usage of an anonymous function is, e.g.

> group_by(as.tbl(data.frame(aa=c(1,2),bb=c("AA","BB"))),bb) %>% do(function(x) {head(x,1)})
Error: Results are not data frames at positions: 1, 2

but it seems that "do" really does want a data frame and not a function, since the following works:

> group_by(as.tbl(data.frame(aa=c(1,2),bb=c("AA","BB"))),bb) %>% do(head(.,1))
Source: local data frame [2 x 2]
Groups: bb

  aa bb
1  1 AA
2  2 BB

I think this may trip up a lot of people, especially those transitioning from plyr. I'm not sure how you'd
emphasize this fact in the documentation, but it's worth a try.

