I have to say I agree with this request, as the following line does not seem to be true in general:
"You can use do to perform arbitrary computation, returning either a data frame or arbitrary objects which will be stored in a list."

It's the last part of that sentence I wonder about. In my reply to another thread this morning,
I created a function that returned a data frame by coercing a numeric vector to a list. This
was necessary because returning a numeric vector threw an error. In plyr, this works:

library(plyr)
library(dplyr)

DFm <- mtcars %>% select(mpg, disp:vs) %>% gather("var", "val", -vs)

f2 <- function(DF)
{
   pv <- t.test(val ~ vs, data = DF)[["p.value"]]
   means <- tapply(DF$val, DF$vs, mean)
   sds <- tapply(DF$val, DF$vs, sd)
   stats <- c(means, sds, pv)
   names(stats) <- c("mean0", "mean1", "sd0", "sd1", "p.value")
   stats
}

plyr::ddply(DFm, "var", f2)

# whereas
> DFm %>% group_by("var") %>% do(f2(.))

Error: Results are not data frames at positions: 1

Changing the last line of f2() to
data.frame(as.list(stats))

and re-running the modified f2() through the pipeline works.

Ramming the original f2() through the pipeline should be equivalent to dlply()
but it clearly is not, so either I'm missing something or the present documentation is
partially inaccurate. The error message is clear enough and I modified the
function accordingly, but the documentation suggests that I should be
able to return a list of vectors and dplyr will coerce the result into a data frame
a la plyr::ldply().

Perhaps my confusion is this:  the documentation is accurate as far as the *result*
produced from do(), but what must the function on which do() operates return?

Dennis

